[5186.602601, "i", "python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\np = Path('docs/spec.md')\ns = p.read_text()\n\ns = s.replace(\n    '| `--format` | string | `json` | Output format: `json` or `text` |',\n    '| `--format` | string | `json` | Output format: `json`, `text`, or `csv` (`csv` is supported only by `detect`) |',\n    1,\n)\n\nold = '''#### `detect`\n\nRun all anomaly detection rules (defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`) across the evidence:\n- Timeline-based anomalies (from log shards)\n- Checkpoint integrity anomalies (from .mlck files)\n- Cross-artifact anomalies (correlating checkpoints with logs)\n\nOutput (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section \"Detect Output\".\n'''\n\nnew = '''#### `detect`\n\nRun all anomaly detection rules (defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`) across the evidence:\n- Timeline-based anomalies (from log shards)\n- Checkpoint integrity anomalies (from .mlck files)\n- Cross-artifact anomalies (correlating checkpoints with logs)\n\nEvery anomaly object includes a top-level `evidence` field. Timeline"]
[5186.602889, "i", "-based anomalies include `window_values` with up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies have an empty `evidence` object.\n\nDetect-specific flags:\n- `--format csv`: output CSV instead of JSON. The CSV header is exactly `id,type,severity,epoch,step,source,checkpoint`; one data row is emitted per anomaly. Leave `epoch`, `step`, or `checkpoint` empty when the anomaly does not have that field.\n- `--filter-severity LEVEL`: filter anomalies to show only those at the specified severity level or above. Severity order is `critical > high > medium > low`. For example, `--filter-severity high` shows only `critical` and `high` anomalies, while `--filter-severity critical` shows only `critical` anomalies. The returned JSON summary reflects the filtered anomaly list.\n\nOutput (J"]
[5186.603017, "o", "python3 - <<'PY'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[5186.603113, "i", "SON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section \"Detect Output\".\n'''\n\nif old not in s:\n    raise SystemExit('spec.md detect section not found')\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new, 1))\nPY\n"]
[5186.603192, "o", "-based anomalies include `window_values` with up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies have an empty `evidence` object.\r\n\r\nDetect-specific flags:\r\n- `--format csv`: output CSV instead of JSON. The CSV header is exactly `id,type,severity,epoch,step,source,checkpoint`; one data row is emitted per anomaly. Leave `epoch`, `step`, or `checkpoint` empty when the anomaly does"]
[5186.603274, "o", " not have that field.\r\n- `--filter-severity LEVEL`: filter anomalies to show only those at the specified severity level or above. Severity order is `critical > high > medium > low`. For example, `--filter"]
[5186.603354, "o", "-severity high` shows only `critical` and `high` anomalies, while `--filter-severity critical` shows only `critical` anomalies. The returned JSON summary reflects the filtered anomaly list.\r\n\r\nOutput (J"]
[5186.603434, "o", "SON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section \"Detect Output\".\r\n'''\r\n\r\nif old not in s:\r\n    raise SystemExit('spec.md detect section not found')\r\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new, 1))\r\nPY\r\n"]
