[5186.613981, "o", "Every anomaly object includes a top-level `evidence` field. Timeline-based anomalies include `window_values` with up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies have an empty `evidence` object.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[5186.614077, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> \r\n"]
[5186.614152, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[5186.614215, "o", "> "]
[5186.614289, "o", "Detect-specific flags:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[5186.614357, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[5186.615743, "o", "- `--format csv`: output CSV instead of JSON. The CSV header is exactly `id,type,severity,epoch,step,source,checkpoint`; one data row is emitted per anomaly. Leave `epoch`, `step`, or `checkpoint` empty when the anomaly does not have that field.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[5186.615815, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[5186.618105, "o", "- `--filter-severity LEVEL`: filter anomalies to show only those at the specified severity level or above. Severity order is `critical > high > medium > low`. For example, `--filter-severity high` shows only `critical` and `high` anomalies, while `--filter-severity critical` shows only `critical` anomalies. The returned JSON summary reflects the filtered anomaly list.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
