[5882.443367, "o", "docs/analysis_rules.md:36:- **Severity**: `high` if Z-score > 5.0, else `medium`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:45:- **Severity**: `critical` if increase > 5.0 (500%), else `high`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:55:- **Severity**: `medium`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:62:- **Severity**: `high`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:90:- **Severity**: `high`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:97:- **Severity**: `low`\r\ndocs/analysis_rules.md:104:- **Severity**: `high`\r\ndocs/output_schema.md:223:- `summary`: reflects the filtered anomaly list when `--filter-severity` is supplied.\r\ndocs/output_schema.md:234:One header row is followed by one data row per anomaly. Each data row has 7 comma-separated fields in that order. Leave `epoch`, `step`, or `checkpoint` empty when the anomaly does not have that field. Severity filtering with `--filter-severity LEVEL` applies before CSV output, so the rows reflect only the filtered anomaly list.\r\ndocs/spec.md:80:- `--filter-severity LEVEL`: filter anomalies to show only those at the specified severity level or above. Seve"]
[5882.443512, "o", "rity order is `critical > high > medium > low > info`. For example, `--filter-severity high` shows only `critical` and `high` anomalies, while `--filter-severity critical` shows only `critical` anomalies. The returned JSON summary reflects the filtered anomaly list.\r\ninternal/forensics/detect.go:255:\t\t\t\tSeverity: \"info\",\r\ninternal/forensics/detect.go:290:\t\t\t\tSeverity: \"info\",\r\ninternal/forensics/detect.go:329:\t\t\t\tSeverity: \"high\",\r\ninternal/forensics/detect.go:423:\t\t\t\t\tSeverity: \"medium\",\r\ninternal/forensics/detect.go:507:\t\t\t\tSeverity: \"high\",\r\n"]
