[6082.446741, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[6082.4504, "o", "## 2. Anomaly Detection Rules\r\n\r\nAnomalies are classified by severity: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`.\r\n\r\nSeverity order, from most severe to least severe, is `critical` > `high` > `medium` > `low` > `info`. Anomalies are sorted by severity, then `type` alphabetically, then `epoch` ascending, then `step` ascending.\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Gradient Norm Spike\r\n"]
[6082.450491, "o", "\r\n"]
[6082.450983, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[6082.451055, "o", "root@n30009:/app# "]
[6082.736226, "i", "sed -n '214,228p' docs/output_schema.md\n"]
[6082.736561, "o", "sed -n '214,228p' docs/output_schema.md\r\n"]
[6082.736691, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[6082.739467, "o", "**Field rules:**\r\n- `id`: Sequential ID in format `ANOM-NNN`, assigned in detection order.\r\n- `anomalies`: sorted by severity (`critical` > `high` > `medium` > `low` > `info`), then `type` alphabetically, then `epoch` ascending, then `step` ascending.\r\n- Severity order is `critical` > `high` > `medium` > `low` > `info`. `--filter-severity LEVEL` returns anomalies at the specified severity level or above: `critical` returns only `critical`; `high` returns `critical` and `high`; `medium` returns `critical`, `high`, and `medium`; `low` returns `critical`, `high`, `medium`, and `low`; `info` returns all severities.\r\n- `type` values: `gradient_spike`, `loss_divergence`, `lr_anomaly`, `metric_regression`, `tensor_checksum_mismatch`, `header_checksum_mismatch`, `signature_invalid`, `provenance_chain_break`, `training_gap`, `dataset_hash_inconsistency`.\r\n- `details`: type-specific fields as defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`.\r\n"]
[6082.739583, "o", "- `evidence`: sibling of `id`, `type`, `severity`, `details`, and `source`; never nested inside `details`. Timeline-based anomalies include `window_values`, an array of up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of anomaly detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies, such as `tensor_checksum_mismatch`, must have an empty evidence object: `{}`.\r\n- `source`: the artifact file where the anomaly was detected.\r\n- `epoch` and `step`: present only for timeline-based anomalies.\r\n- `checkpoint`: present when the anomaly is related to a specific checkpoint file; omitted for timeline-based anomalies that are not checkpoint-specific.\r\n- `summary.by_severity` includes counts only for severities present in the returned anomaly list, including `info` when info-level anomalies are returned.\r\n- `summary`: reflects the filtere"]
[6082.739665, "o", "d anomaly list when `--filter-severity` is supplied.\r\n- If no anomalies are detected, `anomalies` is an empty array.\r\n\r\n### Detect CSV Output\r\n"]
[6082.740072, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[6082.740141, "o", "root@n30009:/app# "]
